WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.182
Resistance questions often go wrong because the learner quietly moves the property from the route to the source.

2
00:00:05.182 --> 00:00:10.363
But resistance belongs to the path that the carriers must travel through.

3
00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:15.545
A longer route usually means more opposition.

4
00:00:15.545 --> 00:00:20.727
A wider route usually means less opposition.

5
00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:25.908
Material matters too.

6
00:00:25.908 --> 00:00:31.090
The best starting habit is to describe the path before using the word resistance.

7
00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:36.272
Once the route story is clear, the ranking questions in this lesson become much more stable.

8
00:00:36.272 --> 00:00:41.453
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 4 gets safer once the learner keeps resistance attached to the route rather than to the source.

9
00:00:41.453 --> 00:00:46.635
Length means longer route, more opposition.

10
00:00:46.635 --> 00:00:51.817
A longer wire gives carriers more difficult path to travel through.

11
00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:56.998
Width means wider route, less opposition.

12
00:00:56.998 --> 00:01:02.180
A thicker wire gives carriers more room and so reduces the resistance.

13
00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:08.646
This diagram matters because it makes the route comparisons visible.

14
00:01:08.646 --> 00:01:15.112
The carriers have farther to travel in a longer wire, and that tends to increase the opposition.

15
00:01:15.112 --> 00:01:21.579
A wider path gives carriers more room, so the resistance drops.

16
00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:28.045
The source can stay the same while the route resistance changes because the route owns that property.

17
00:01:28.045 --> 00:01:34.511
That is the picture the learner needs before ranking wires from greatest resistance to least.

18
00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:40.977
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong resistance answer starts by comparing the route before touching the circuit source.

19
00:01:40.977 --> 00:01:47.444
Long route: A longer wire usually gives greater resistance when the other factors are unchanged.

20
00:01:47.444 --> 00:01:53.910
Wide route: A thicker wire usually gives lower resistance for the same material and length.

21
00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:58.778
A hallway analogy helps because it makes resistance feel like path difficulty.

22
00:01:58.778 --> 00:02:03.646
If people move through a longer corridor, they face more opportunity for slowing and crowding.

23
00:02:03.646 --> 00:02:08.515
If the corridor becomes wider, movement becomes easier.

24
00:02:08.515 --> 00:02:13.383
The front door sign did not create the drag.

25
00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:18.251
The layout did.

26
00:02:18.251 --> 00:02:23.119
Circuits work the same way at this level: the source may drive the circuit, but the resistance belongs to the material and geometry of the route itself.

27
00:02:23.119 --> 00:02:27.987
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy helps the learner trust that resistance belongs to the path the carriers must use.

28
00:02:27.987 --> 00:02:32.855
Long corridor means more drag.

29
00:02:32.855 --> 00:02:37.724
A longer path gives more opportunity for resistance-like opposition.

30
00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:42.592
Wide corridor means less drag.

31
00:02:42.592 --> 00:02:47.460
A wider path allows easier movement, matching lower resistance.

32
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:52.718
At this stage the most important rules are comparative.

33
00:02:52.718 --> 00:02:57.977
Longer route means greater resistance when the material and width stay the same.

34
00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:03.235
Wider route means lower resistance when the material and length stay the same.

35
00:03:03.235 --> 00:03:08.494
Material changes matter too, because some substances oppose carrier motion more than others.

36
00:03:08.494 --> 00:03:13.752
The learner does not need a complicated microscopic derivation here.

37
00:03:13.752 --> 00:03:19.011
What matters is owning the route-first logic before using resistance inside later circuit formulas.

38
00:03:19.011 --> 00:03:24.269
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works once the learner can explain why the same source can drive routes with different resistance values.

39
00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:29.528
Length rule: Hold material and width fixed before using the longer-route comparison.

40
00:03:29.528 --> 00:03:34.786
Width rule: Hold material and length fixed before using the wider-route comparison.

41
00:03:34.786 --> 00:03:40.045
longer route -> greater resistance.

42
00:03:40.045 --> 00:03:45.303
wider route -> lower resistance.

43
00:03:45.303 --> 00:03:50.562
Describe the path first.

44
00:03:50.562 --> 00:03:55.820
Then say how the resistance compares.

45
00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:01.756
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

46
00:04:01.756 --> 00:04:07.692
First identify which route property changed: length, width, material, or more than one of these.

47
00:04:07.692 --> 00:04:13.628
Second compare the routes one factor at a time.

48
00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:19.564
Third rank the resistances and justify the order in words tied to the route geometry.

49
00:04:19.564 --> 00:04:25.500
This routine keeps the explanation physical and stops the battery from being blamed for a property that belongs to the wire.

50
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.436
Keep this takeaway in view: Route first, comparison second, ranking third is the safest routine for Lesson 4.

51
00:04:31.436 --> 00:04:37.372
Step 1 means spot the route change.

52
00:04:37.372 --> 00:04:43.308
Name whether length, width, or material changed across the compared wires.

53
00:04:43.308 --> 00:04:49.244
Step 2 means compare one factor at a time.

54
00:04:49.244 --> 00:04:55.180
Use the length and width rules carefully instead of jumping straight to the ranking.

55
00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:01.823
A common weak sentence says the battery makes the resistance bigger.

56
00:05:01.823 --> 00:05:08.466
This is exactly the misconception to clear before the worked example begins.

57
00:05:08.466 --> 00:05:15.109
The source can provide a certain voltage across the loop, but the resistance is owned by the route itself.

58
00:05:15.109 --> 00:05:21.752
If the wire changes from short and thick to long and thin on the same battery, the resistance changes because the path changed.

59
00:05:21.752 --> 00:05:28.395
That distinction will matter even more in the next lesson when voltage and resistance together set the current.

60
00:05:28.395 --> 00:05:35.038
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 4 becomes much safer once the learner keeps route properties and source properties on different sides of the story.

61
00:05:35.038 --> 00:05:41.681
Weak claim means battery made the drag.

62
00:05:41.681 --> 00:05:48.324
This puts the route property onto the wrong circuit component.

63
00:05:48.324 --> 00:05:54.967
Correction means wire owns the resistance.

64
00:05:54.967 --> 00:06:01.610
Length, width, material, and temperature belong to the route side of the story.

65
00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:05.847
Run four checks.

66
00:06:05.847 --> 00:06:10.085
Resistance belongs to the route.

67
00:06:10.085 --> 00:06:14.322
Longer wires usually have greater resistance when the other factors are fixed.

68
00:06:14.322 --> 00:06:18.559
Wider wires usually have lower resistance.

69
00:06:18.559 --> 00:06:22.796
And changing the wire can change the resistance even when the source stays the same.

70
00:06:22.796 --> 00:06:27.034
Those four checks capture the whole lesson structure before the worked example starts.

71
00:06:27.034 --> 00:06:31.271
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can rank resistance by route geometry before thinking about the battery.

72
00:06:31.271 --> 00:06:35.508
Owner means the route.

73
00:06:35.508 --> 00:06:39.745
Keep resistance on the path side of the circuit story.

74
00:06:39.745 --> 00:06:43.983
Length means longer means larger.

75
00:06:43.983 --> 00:06:48.220
Use this when width and material are unchanged.

76
00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:54.117
Start by checking what changed between the wires: length, width, material, or more than one of these.

77
00:06:54.117 --> 00:07:00.015
Then use the route rules to compare the resistance one factor at a time.

78
00:07:00.015 --> 00:07:05.912
After that, state the ranking from greatest to least and justify it in a route-centered sentence.

79
00:07:05.912 --> 00:07:11.809
If the battery stays the same, say so, but do not let it take over the resistance story.

80
00:07:11.809 --> 00:07:17.706
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

81
00:07:17.706 --> 00:07:23.604
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 4 improves immediately once resistance is kept on the route and off the battery.

82
00:07:23.604 --> 00:07:29.501
Do not skip the route description: A resistance ranking is easier once the physical path differences are named.

83
00:07:29.501 --> 00:07:35.398
Do not skip the battery correction: If the source did not change, say so and keep the resistance on the route side.

84
00:07:35.398 --> 00:07:41.295
identify the route changes first.

85
00:07:41.295 --> 00:07:47.193
compare length, width, and material carefully.

86
00:07:47.193 --> 00:07:53.090
The strongest answers sound like wire comparisons, not like source comparisons.
